  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Given that $f(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2},$ solve $f(x)\\le 0.$\n\n$x\\in {$\n{{field-1}}$}$\n$\u22c3[$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$]$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve the inequality $f(x)\\le 0,$ we need to find the values of $x$ for which the curve $y=f(x)$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, we factor the polynomial $f(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-3x+2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}({x}^{2}-x-2x+2) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}[(x(x-1)-2(x-1)] \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-1)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the polynomial are $x=0$, $x=1$ and $x=2,$ where\n\n- $x=0$ has multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $x=1$ and $x=2$ are simple roots.\n\nSo, we can graph the curve as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007649/assets/baf599e732aa524409881d4bc7598c3f998cffe116ca1cdcd76fb6e6bd49a078.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving $f(x)\\le 0,$ we look for the part of the curve that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is\n\n$$\nx\\in {0}\u22c3[1,2].\n$$",
